              %li
                %a{:href => "#details"} Details
              %li
                %a{:href => "#literature_and_links"} Literature and links
          %h2#idea Idea
          %p
            A
            %em Grothendieck connection
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mo ∇
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \nabla
            is a way to encode the parallel transport of a
            %em flat
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/connection+on+a+bundle"} connection
            along
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/infinitesimal+object"} infinitesimal
            paths.
          %p
            In the language of
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/schreiber/show/%E2%88%9E-Lie+theory"} ∞-Lie theory
            it is a morphism from the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/infinitesimal+path+%E2%88%9E-groupoid"} infinitesimal path ∞-groupoid
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msub
                    %mi Π
                    %mi inf
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi X
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mo ≔
                  %mi ℑ
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi X
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \Pi_{inf}(X) \coloneqq \Im(X)
            of a suitable
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/space"} space
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi X
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} X
            (a
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/scheme"} scheme
            in
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/algebraic+geometry"} algebraic geometry
            or, more generally, a space in
            = succeed ")" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/synthetic+differential+geometry"} synthetic differential geometry
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Lie+%E2%88%9E-groupoid"} Lie ∞-groupoid
            = succeed ":" do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi A
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} A
          .maruku-equation
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mo ∇
                  %mo :
                  %msub
                    %mi Π
                    %mi inf
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi X
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mo →
                  %mi A
                  %mspace{:width => "thinmathspace"}
                  %mo .
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"}
                  \\nabla : \Pi_{inf}(X) \to A
                  \\,.
          %p
            Here
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msub
                    %mi Π
                    %mi inf
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi X
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \Pi_{inf}(X)
            is effectively the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/infinitesimal+singular+simplicial+complex"} infinitesimal singular simplicial complex
            of
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi X
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} X
            and
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi A
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} A
            is modeled typically as a
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/sheaf"} sheaf
            of
            = succeed "s" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/%E2%88%9E-groupoid"} ∞-groupoid
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/models+for+%E2%88%9E-stack+%28%E2%88%9E%2C1%29-toposes"} models for ∞-stack (∞,1)-toposes
          %p
            This was originally considered by
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Alexander+Grothendieck"} Grothendieck
            for
            = succeed "s" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/scheme"} scheme
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/algebraic+geometry"} algebraic geometry
            for the case that
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi A
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} A
            is a 1-
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/groupoid"} groupoid
            – but the generalization to
            = succeed "s" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/%E2%88%9E-groupoid"} ∞-groupoid
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/synthetic+differential+geometry"} synthetic differential geometry
            is immediate.
          %h2#details Details
          %p
            In his study of
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/crystalline+cohomology"} crystalline cohomology
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Alexander+Grothendieck"} Grothendieck
            has noticed that flat
            = succeed "s" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/connection+on+a+bundle"} connection
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/descent"} descent
            data for
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/deRham+complex"} deRham
            descent; he called them the costratification of the
            = succeed "-th" do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi n
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} n
            = succeed "-the" do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi n
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} n
          %p
            Already in EGA, Grothendieck has introduced a notion of
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/regular+differential+operator"} regular differential operator
            and of jet-spaces, which were later by Malgrange and Spencer transferred into the study of differential equations and analytic deformation theory.
          %p
            De Rham descent for a relative
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/scheme"} scheme
            (= morphism of schemes)
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi f
                  %mo :
                  %mi X
                  %mo →
                  %mi S
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} f: X\to S
            is formulated in terms of resolutions or
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/infinitesimal+object"} infinitesimal
            thickenings of the diagonal for the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/simplicial+object"} simplicial
            scheme
          .maruku-equation
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi ⋯
                  %mi X
                  %msub
                    %mo ×
                    %mi S
                  %mi X
                  %msub
                    %mo ×
                    %mi S
                  %mi X
                  %mover
                    %mover
                      %mo →
                      %mo →
                    %mo →
                  %mi X
                  %msub
                    %mo ×
                    %mi S
                  %mi X
                  %mover
                    %mo →
                    %mo →
                  %mi X
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"}
                  \\cdots
                  X
                  \\times_S X \times_S X
                  \\stackrel{\to}{\stackrel{\to}{\to}}
                  X \times_S X \stackrel{\to}{\to}
                  X
          %p
            corresponding to
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi f
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} f
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi X
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} X
            is a
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/smooth+scheme"} smooth scheme
            of finite type over
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi S
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} S
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi Δ
                  %mo :
                  %mi X
                  %mo ↪
                  %mi X
                  %msub
                    %mo ×
                    %mi S
                  %mi X
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \Delta: X\hookrightarrow X\times_S X
            is a
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/closed+subscheme"} closed immersion of schemes
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi ℐ
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathcal{I}
            be the corresponding defining ideal sheaf (locally it is generated by the elements of the form
            = succeed "," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi t
                    %mo ⊗
                    %mn 1
                    %mo −
                    %mn 1
                    %mo ⊗
                    %mi t
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} t\otimes 1 - 1\otimes t
            = succeed ")." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/K%C3%A4hler+differential"} Kähler differential
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msup
                    %mi ℐ
                    %mrow
                      %mi n
                      %mo +
                      %mn 1
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathcal{I}^{n+1}
            defines for the
            = succeed "-th" do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi n
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} n
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msup
                    %mi X
                    %mrow
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                      %mi n
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} X^{(n)}
            and the diagonal subscheme
            = succeed ";" do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi X
                    %mo ≅
                    %mi Δ
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi X
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %mo ↪
                    %mi X
                    %msub
                      %mo ×
                      %mi S
                    %mi X
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} X\cong \Delta(X)\hookrightarrow X\times_S X
          .maruku-equation
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi X
                  %mo ↪
                  %msup
                    %mi X
                    %mrow
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                      %mn 1
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mo ↪
                  %msup
                    %mi X
                    %mrow
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                      %mn 2
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mo ↪
                  %mi …
                  %mo ↪
                  %msup
                    %mi X
                    %mrow
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                      %mn ∞
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mo =
                  %mover
                    %mi X
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ^
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"}
                  X\hookrightarrow X^{(1)}\hookrightarrow X^{(2)}\hookrightarrow \ldots \hookrightarrow X^{(\infty)}=\hat{X}
          %p
            where
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mover
                    %mi X
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ^
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \hat{X}
            is the completion (the corresponding
            = succeed ")." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/formal+scheme"} formal scheme
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi ℱ
                  %mo →
                  %mi Sch
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathcal{F}\to Sch
            be a
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Grothendieck+fibration"} Grothendieck fibration
            over the category of schemes (or the subcategory of the category of schemes containing all schemes in our consideration) classified under the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Grothendieck+construction"} Grothendieck construction
            by some
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/pseudofunctor"} pseudofunctor
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi A
                    %mo :
                    %msup
                      %mi Sch
                      %mi op
                    %mo →
                    %mi Cat
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} A : Sch^{op} \to Cat
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/stack"} stack
            of
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/quasicoherent+sheaves"} quasicoherent sheaves
            of
            = succeed "-modules." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi 𝒪
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathcal{O}
          %p
            Consider now the projections
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msub
                      %mi d
                      %mn 0
                    %mo ,
                    %msub
                      %mi d
                      %mn 1
                    %mo :
                    %mi X
                    %msub
                      %mo ×
                      %mi S
                    %mi X
                    %mo →
                    %mi X
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} d_0,d_1:X\times_S X\to X
          .un_defn
            %h6#definition_grothendieck_connection Definition (Grothendieck connection)
            %p
              A
              %strong Grothendieck connection
              on an object
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi ρ
                    %mo ∈
                    %mi ℱ
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \rho\in\mathcal{F}
              is a
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/descent"} descent
              datum of the form
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi ρ
                    %mo ,
                    %mi θ
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} (\rho,\theta)
              where
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi θ
                    %mo :
                    %msubsup
                      %mi d
                      %mn 0
                      %mo *
                    %mi ρ
                    %mo →
                    %msubsup
                      %mi d
                      %mn 1
                      %mo *
                    %mi ρ
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \theta: d_0^*\rho\to d_1^*\rho
              is an isomorphism satisfying the cocycle (and the normalization) condition and such that the restriction (pullback along inclusion) on the diagonal is the identity.
          %p
            The constructions can be almost literally transferred to the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/synthetic+differential+geometry"} synthetic differential geometry
            using
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/infinitesimal+object"} infinitesimal neighborhoods
            in that setup.
          %p
            More recently, some partial generalizations were found in the purely algebraic framework. One could take any
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/coring"} coring
            (or even an additive
            = succeed ")" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/comonad"} comonad
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/grouplike+element"} grouplike element
            and define corresponding connections and descent data and generalize the correspondence found by Grothendieck (see
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/semi-free+dga"} semi-free dga
            and Menini-Stefan reference below).
